Falsifies `capability_table::the_free_mask_is_the_empty_slots`.

The defect: `empty` takes the capability out and decrements the count, and does not
set the slot's free bit. A slot freed this way is empty in the array and occupied in
the words, so `insert` never hands it out again: the table leaks one slot per delete
until the thread dies. On the kernel's `CALL` path, where every call mints a Reply
and every reply deletes it, a server would run out of slots after a few dozen
requests and start refusing calls with `NoFreeSlot`.

It is the plausible mistake because the words are new and the count beside it is old:
a reviewer checking `empty` sees the `take` and the decrement it always had.

Expected to fail: `assert!(is_free_set_right_at(&cs, position), "wrong after
delete_matching")`, on any table state where the sweep frees an occupied slot. The
assertion before it passes, because `put` goes through `fill`, which is untouched.

diff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs
--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -423,7 +423,6 @@
         /// what a boot holds, not what it reached.
         pub(super) fn empty(&mut self, slot: usize) -> Option<Capability<O>> {
             let capability = self.slots.get_mut(slot)?.take()?;
-            self.free[slot / WORD_BITS] |= 1u64 << (slot % WORD_BITS);
             self.used -= 1;
             Some(capability)
         }
